  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 330 PM EDT TUE AUG 18 2026\n\nAt the onset of the extended forecast period, large-scale 500 mb \ntroughing remains anchored across much of the eastern CONUS. In \nthe pre-dawn hours Thursday, a nocturnal 40-50 kt low-level jet \n(LLJ) oriented across western and central Kentucky will coincide \nwith a shortwave passing through the Ohio Valley. While the LLJ \nweakens to 30-40 kts as it shifts into eastern Kentucky, rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms are expected through Thursday. The \nfirst round will move through during the morning hours, favoring \nareas across the Cumberland, Southern Licking, and Big Sandy River\nbasins near the TN, VA, and WV borders. A brief lull in activity \nis expected before redevelopment occurs early Thursday afternoon \nalong the I-64 corridor, propagating south through the remainder \nof the day. Models indicate drier air working in aloft by late \nafternoon, which could suppress rain chances. However, the \nenvironment will support PWAT values near 1.75 inches (80th to \n85th percentile of climatology). Given saturated soils from recent\nrainfall, isolated instances of minor flooding remain possible \nThursday through Thursday night, supporting WPCs Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall. Adequate storm motion \nshould keep activity progressive, limiting widespread flash \nflooding potential.\n\nAs the frontal boundary associated with Thursdays convection lingers\nnear the area on Friday, shower and thunderstorm chances will \npersist, with the highest probabilities during the afternoon along\nand south of the Hal Rogers Parkway / KY Highway 80 corridor. \nBeyond Friday, models and ensemble guidance remain in broad \nagreement on a continued upper-level pattern featuring a ridge \nover the western CONUS and troughing across the east, though \namplitude differences remain. Another shortwave embedded within \nthe longwave trough is projected to dig out of the Upper Midwest \ninto the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley Saturday into Sunday. \nMultiple 00Z AI-based guidance suites from August 18th show \nconditional severe potential on Saturday, primarily centered over \nSW Pennsylvania, with lower probabilities (5-15%) extending \nsouthwest into eastern Kentucky. At present, PoPs support isolated\nto scattered coverage across the Licking and Big Sandy basins on \nSaturday. Isolated shower or storm activity may linger across the \nBig Sandy early Sunday morning; otherwise, dry weather is expected\nto return through Monday morning. By Monday afternoon, guidance \ndepicts another upper-level low\u2014which a few models attempt to \nclose off\u2014digging southeast from the Upper Midwest into the\nNorthern Great Lakes. This feature could bring another round of \nshower and thunderstorm chances to the area on Tuesday.",
